WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.440
“Help me!

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.840
Would somebody please help me?

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.160
I’m lost and I can’t see!”

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.000
It echoed through the alley in a thin, reedy
voice that suggested brittleness of body and

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:12.720
spirit.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.080
Who wouldn’t hear that cry and feel a pang
of sympathy?

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.880
Are you so heartless that you’d ignore the
desperate pleas for help of a blind man, clearly

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:21.840
in distress?

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.720
For all you know, he might even be in danger.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.680
What if he walks into a road and gets hit
by a car?

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.840
Would you really want that on your conscience?

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.880
Questions like these were flying through the
head of the Good Samaritan when he decided

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.280
to go and investigate, when it seemed like
everyone else was just content to pretend

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.640
they didn’t hear it and carry on with their
day as normal.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.000
The Good Samaritan was a person not unlike
yourself.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.560
They were also the kind of person we all hoped
and wished we would be in a situation such

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:51.440
as this.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.280
Kindness and generosity is a virtue, after
all.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.760
The problem is, there are people out there
who know this, and are willing to take advantage

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.240
of that kindness and generosity.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.960
But even worse is the fact that there are
things that are definitely not people out

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.920
there willing to take&nbsp;
advantage of human compassion,&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:10.640
too.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.560
But back to the Good Samaritan.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.200
He approached the sound of the distressed
man in the alley: He looked old and frail,

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.520
hunched over with the weight of his years.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.480
He wore thick, dark glasses, and his white
cane was laying on the road a few feet away

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:25.280
from him.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.000
It was a tragic scene, but thankfully, the
Good Samaritan was here to help.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.080
He approached the old man, calmly telling
him that it was okay.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.720
The Good Samaritan picked up the walking cane
and passed it over to the blind old man.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.520
His face spread into a warm smile, and he
said.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.200
“Thank you, thank you, thank you, young
man.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.800
It warms my heart to know there are still
good people like you on this earth.”

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.440
The old man told the Good Samaritan that he’d
come into town to get some fresh air and a

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.720
little exercise, at which point he’d gotten
lost.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.120
When the Good Samaritan asked whether it was
safe for him to come alone, the old man told

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.920
him that he always used to go on walks with
his wife, but ever since the accident, she

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.560
didn’t seem to want to be seen in public.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.600
The Good Samaritan naturally felt for the
old man, who seemed as sweet as he was feeble.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.000
He offered to escort the old man back to his
house personally, wanting to make sure that

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.680
he returned home safely.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.800
The old man smiled and graciously accepted,
saying that his wife would probably want to

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.840
thank the kind young man for helping him in
person.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.640
Of course, the Good Samaritan was so wrapped
up in the warm glow of doing a good deed that

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.880
he never once stopped to question the strangeness
of this whole situation.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:44.480
He just walked along with the old man, listening
to him tell his fanciful stories, as they

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.680
left the city center and crept further into
the sleepy outskirts of their small Kentucky

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:49.840
town.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.600
He was just rambling on, as old men tend to.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.640
He told the Good Samaritan a little more about
his beloved wife: She was a kind woman whom

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.120
he’d known for as long as he could remember.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.840
His elementary school sweetheart, then his
high school girlfriend, and not long after

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.960
they graduated, his beautiful, blushing bride.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.600
Oh yes, she’d always been the prettiest
lady, until the accident.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.160
Curiosity was getting the better of him: The
Good Samaritan couldn’t help but ask what

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.280
exactly the “accident” he kept alluding
to was, and the old man was all too happy

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:24.640
to give him an answer.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.080
Not that this answer was, in any way, pleasant.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.960
His wife had been trapped in a terrible fire
in town that gave her severe burns all over

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:33.920
her body.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.960
She was lucky to be alive, but the flames
took her beauty and her confidence away from

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:39.600
her.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.400
Since then, she’d become a real homebody,
often refusing to leave the house, and rarely

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.160
ever letting strangers see her.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.440
The Good Samaritan’s heart ached for this
poor old couple.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.280
Why were such nice people often forced to
endure so much suffering?

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.160
Did this old man even have anyone else to
talk to?

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.800
They reached his house soon enough, it was
the furthest one on the end of the street,

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.040
right next to the darkness of the woods.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.760
It looked old and dilapidated, but it wasn’t
like the old blind man could even know that.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.800
As they were walking up the small set of stairs
to the porch, the old man stumbled slightly,

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.600
causing his thick, dark glasses to fall to
the floor.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.760
The Good Samaritan reflexively leaned over
to pick up the glasses for the old man, and

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.120
as he passed them back, he noticed something
strange.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.840
The old man wasn’t just blind, he literally
didn’t have any eyes.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.840
Just two black, empty sockets where his eyes
should be.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:35.680
The Good Samaritan was just thankful that
the old man wasn’t able to see him staring

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:37.600
as he put the glasses back on.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.360
The two of them entered the house.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.120
The Good Samaritan was too busy thinking about
the old man’s empty eye sockets to even

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:48.640
notice him locking the door behind them, and
by then, it was already too late for him.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:52.480
The old man called out into the dark expanse
of the house.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:55.840
“Sweetheart, I’ve got you a live one!”

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:00.960
Before the Good Samaritan could ask what he
actually meant by that, the old man shuffled

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.920
off, deftly navigating the halls through memory
alone, and took one of the two seats at the

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:06.880
dining table.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.440
He just sat there, smiling.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.320
Waiting.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:14.080
That’s when the Good Samaritan heard a creaking
sound coming from above.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:19.520
Something moving, up in the darkness of the
second floor, and then descending the stairs

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:22.160
with heavy, clomping footsteps.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:27.200
When his eyes came into focus in the dark,
he saw it there, the most terrifying thing

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.720
he’d ever witnessed, coming down the stairs
towards him.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.320
It was vaguely human in its shape alone, but
in all other respects, it was a monster through

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:37.120
and through.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:43.120
This gnarled, twisted creature with bulging
eyes, dead flesh, and filthy exposed fangs.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.920
Like it’d stepped straight out of a nightmare
and into the physical world, where it did

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.040
not belong.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:54.800
There looked to be a huge hole in its lower
torso, too, with no blood or organs inside.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.960
Just an infinite blackness.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.560
The creature’s lipless mouth twisted into
a smile.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:04.640
The creature said something in a voice that
the Good Samaritan couldn’t understand.

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:10.000
It was the Black Tongue, something ancient,
terrifying, and unknown.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.560
As the creature got closer and closer, the
Good Samaritan turned and made a run for the

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:15.040
door.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:19.520
He tried to figure out the lock, his hands
fumbling and uncoordinated by fear.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:23.920
He could hear the footsteps and the heavy
breathing of that thing getting closer behind

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:24.320
him.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:27.280
He was so close...Almost there...

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:31.680
That’s when a strange, black substance seemed
to encase the world around him.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.640
It separated him from the door he was so nearly
able to breach, locking him in.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:42.000
He was trapped inside what looked like a giant,
black sphere made of some unknown material

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:42.960
or energy.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:47.680
He turned to see that monster standing right
behind him, just grinning and waiting for

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:50.640
the inevitability of the situation to sink
in.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.560
The Good Samaritan had never been more terrified.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.280
Something else began to shift inside the sphere.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:03.120
A structure was growing out of the ground,
up and around him, like a cage within a cage.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:08.560
He was trapped and huddled inside a strange
conical structure, as more figures entered

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.000
the sphere around him.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:14.800
They were just like the first, this horrible
horde of desiccated not-people.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.520
All with grinning, lipless mouths.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:22.640
When they closed in on him, the Good Samaritan
had the one consolation that at least it was

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:23.280
over.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:24.640
But it wasn’t.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:25.680
Not yet.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.880
The blind old man waited in the kitchen for
five hours as unmentionable horrors unfolded

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.400
inside the black sphere.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.800
The Good Samaritan was actually lucky, all
in all, because some sessions for victims

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:40.720
inside the sphere had lasted as long as 27
hours.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.600
He screamed the entire time, with the sphere
finally de-manifesting just moments after

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:47.040
the screaming stopped.

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:52.000
Remaining, was only one of the creatures,
but no Good Samaritan.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:56.720
Just several neat piles of human organs, arranged
by organ type.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.560
“Are you done in there, honey?” the blind
old man asked.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.960
“I’ve been getting awfully hungry.”

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:05.760
The creature replied, in perfect English.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.200
“Yes, dear.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:13.840
I’ll fix you up some supper before you go
to bed.”

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.240
The creature picked up some of the discarded
organs and took them into the kitchen, where

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.200
it began cooking them into a stew for the
old man.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:26.960
He ate the stew not long after, blissfully
ignorant to its contents, and went to sleep.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.360
Whatever organs from the Good Samaritan weren’t
used in the stew were simply thrown into the

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.320
fireplace to burn away into ashes.

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:40.480
Later that night, the old man would die peacefully
of a heart attack in his sleep at age 81.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:45.600
He spent the latter ten years of his life
in service of SCP-957.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.080
He’d never even had a wife, and when the
monster first invaded his home, he wasn’t

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:51.120
even blind.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:57.360
He’d simply come home one day to find SCP-957
lurking in his home, waiting for him.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:02.800
As far as the SCP Foundation is aware, it
always targets people who live alone.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.680
The man had tried to resist, but the creature
was freakishly strong in spite of its wiry

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:07.840
frame.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:12.960
It grabbed him and forced his body into the
dark, gaping chasm of its chest.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.920
He was swallowed up into another world on
the other side of the void, and returned four

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:20.560
hours later, a changed man.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:27.440
His eyes were gone, and he now firmly believed
that SCP-957 was his beloved, if burn-scarred,

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:29.120
wife of many years.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:34.720
He’d do anything for her: Even heading out
into the world and feigning distress, to lure

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:37.680
people back into her terrible clutches.

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:41.520
That’s how the old man became SCP-957-1.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.040
He wasn’t the first, and wouldn’t be the
last.

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:49.040
With the old man now dead,&nbsp;
SCP-957 would de-manifest&nbsp;

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:51.840
and head off in search of a new victim, invading

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:55.360
their home and repeating the cycle once more
somewhere else.

00:09:55.360 --> 00:10:01.040
The somewhat happy ending is that in one of
these cases, the SCP Foundation somehow got

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:06.960
wise to the baiting activities of SCP-957,
and they were able to contain the creature,

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:11.440
giving it the Keter object class for its frequent
attempts to either get out or lure in new

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:12.240
victims.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:17.840
It’s never been actively hostile to Foundation
researchers and guards, just a little obtuse.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:22.640
Despite the fact that it’s perfectly capable
of speaking fluent English, it prefers to

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:26.800
speak in an unknown, dead language for somewhat
mysterious reasons.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:32.080
Very little is known about the origins or
true nature of SCP-957, but our one brief

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:35.440
glimpse into its interior life does little
to comfort us.

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:41.040
In 2008, the Foundation recorded 957 having
a conversation with an unseen individual in

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:42.320
its native tongue.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:46.720
Foundation linguistic experts have been able
to partially crack the code on what was being

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:48.000
said that day.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:52.240
957 asked the unseen figure how much longer
it would have to stay here.

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:56.800
The figure told it that it would need to wait
there for a while and that its disguise was

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:58.320
extremely convincing.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:04.000
957 complained that being “here” was boring
and tiresome, but the unseen figure urged

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:06.080
957 to remain patient.

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:15.174
It said “We have to let them watch the few
so that the many can move freely.

00:11:15.174 --> 00:11:16.160
We will be done with our research soon enough…”

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:22.800
Now go check out “SCP-1861 - The Crew of
the HMS Wintersheimer” and “SCP-5049 - The

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:28.480
Skin Salesman - Demon Dan's Discount Homunculus
Depot” for more of the most terrifying anomalous

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:29.760
beings out there!

